---
// --- Wreck, masts showing ---------------------------------------------------
// The chart symbol for the only kind of wreck a chart has to argue about: one
// sunk upright in water shallower than her masts are tall, so the tops still
// break the surface. The label's sounding is the depth beside her.
//
// A section, not a portrait: the surface at the top, the bottom at the bottom,
// and the ship between them. Left out, the seabed took the whole reading with
// it -- nothing in the drawing said which way was down and she floated on a
// rule. She is dug into the sand by the stern, so the sand is drawn over her
// and her after end simply stops inside it. Where a hull enters the bottom
// there is no line anybody can draw.
//
// Everything about her is one angle: down by the stern by sixteen degrees, so
// the deck falls aft, the masts rake aft the same amount and the yards lean
// with them. Plumb and evenly spaced they were three crosses on a rule.
---

<svg
  viewBox="0 0 104 68"
  fill="none"
  stroke="currentColor"
  stroke-width="0.6"
  stroke-linecap="round"
  stroke-linejoin="round"
>
  {/* Chart datum. Two strokes well outboard, not a row of them: a second line
      the length of the first is just a doubled rule. */}
  <path d="M10 22h84" stroke-width="0.8" />
  <path d="M13 25.5h7M84 25.5h7" stroke-width="0.4" opacity="0.5" />

  {/* Hull: a squared transom aft, the sheer falling away from it, a straight
      raked stem forward, and a straight run of keel between the forefoot and
      the stern. Both ends have to be different shapes or she is reversible and
      the trim is doing all the work by itself. The keel is what stops her
      reading as a clog: a hull curved end to end has no bottom to sit on.
      Paper-filled, because the danger line runs behind her and a chart symbol
      knocks out what it sits on. */}
  <path
    d="M24.5 47.5 74 34 77.5 30.5 75.5 41Q68 47 58 49.6L37 52.2Q30 52.9 26.5 53Z"
    stroke-width="0.75"
    fill="var(--color-paper)"
  />

  {/* The bottom, over her. Sand as a solid first, then its own top edge:
      one shape would have to stroke the edges of the page as well. */}
  <path
    d="M4 57.8Q12 57 21 52.5Q27 49 34 51.5Q45 54.5 58 56Q76 57.5 90 56.5Q96 56 100 56.8L100 68 4 68Z"
    fill="var(--color-paper)"
    stroke="none"
  />
  <path
    d="M4 57.8Q12 57 21 52.5Q27 49 34 51.5Q45 54.5 58 56Q76 57.5 90 56.5Q96 56 100 56.8"
    stroke-width="0.5"
  />
  {/* Mizzen, main, fore. The mizzen is a bare pole a stroke above the water:
      her spanker gaff is well under. */}
  <path d="M34 45.43 26.74 20M48 41.43 38.16 7M63 37.14 55.96 12.5" stroke-width="0.7" />
  {/* Two yards on the main, one on the fore, none on the mizzen. Spaced up
      near the tops rather than down the mast: a yard low on a long bare pole
      is a flagpole with a crosstree, and the water is where the rest of her
      rigging is. */}
  <path
    d="M31.89 19.61 50.15 14.39M33.2 12.2 45.12 8.8M49.75 18.06 64.17 13.94"
    stroke-width="0.55"
  />

  {/* A danger line is dotted and it follows the danger, so it is a closed loop
      drawn round this wreck rather than an ellipse centred on nothing. Coarse
      dots on purpose: fine ones disappear entirely at 118px, and with them the
      only thing on the mark that says danger. */}
  <path
    d="M17 34C18 19 27 6 44 2C63-1 83 8 89 23C94 37 89 53 74 58C57 63 31 61 22 50C17 44 16 40 17 34Z"
    stroke-width="0.55"
    stroke-dasharray="0.55 3.6"
    opacity="0.8"
  />
</svg>
<span class="chart-mark__label">Wk 32m</span>
